## 1. Objective
Currently, Tenuto Studio 5 only supports static per-note velocities (`.pp` through `.ff` and `.vol(N)`). If a score includes `.cresc`, `.crescendo`, `.dim`, or `.diminuendo`, the modifier is stored as a raw string in `physics.modifiers` and ignored by the audio engine, causing held chords and melodic runs to play at a flat volume.

This contract implements full `.cresc` / `.crescendo` and `.dim` / `.diminuendo` support:
1. `ASTSerializer.ts` parses `.cresc`, `.crescendo`, `.cresc(start, end)`, `.dim`, `.diminuendo`, and `.dim(start, end)` into `physics.swell`, `physics.startVelocity`, and `physics.endVelocity`.
2. `DocumentParser.ts` detects consecutive notes tagged with `.cresc` or `.dim` in a voice and smoothly interpolates their `startVelocity` and `endVelocity` across the entire phrase.
3. `StudioAudioEngine.ts` ramps both live WebAudio `GainNode` / `BiquadFilterNode` envelopes and offline WAV PCM samples continuously from `startAmp` to `endAmp` across the duration of crescendo and diminuendo events.
4. `WebGPUCanvas.tsx` renders a visual dynamic wedge and `<` / `>` hairpin label on crescendo and diminuendo note blocks in the Piano Roll.

---

## 2. Root Cause
1. `src/parser/ASTSerializer.ts` (`parseModifiersToPhysics`) does not parse `cresc`, `crescendo`, `dim`, or `diminuendo` modifiers into velocity ramp fields on `EventPhysics`.
2. `src/audio/StudioAudioEngine.ts` (`scheduleAcousticPianoVoice`, `scheduleSynthVoice`, and `exportWavBlob`) assumes a standard attack-decay envelope on every note rather than ramping upward on crescendos or tapering downward on diminuendos.
3. `src/components/WebGPUCanvas.tsx` renders all note blocks as flat rectangles without indicating dynamic swells.

